---
/**
 * Refunds and cancellations.
 *
 * DRAFT. This is the policy as the brief describes it, written out in full so
 * that Hakop can read a real page and either agree with it or change it. It
 * is brief open decision 7 and it is marked as a draft on the page itself,
 * because a customer should not have to guess which parts are settled.
 *
 * The cutoff time and the days before a bake date are read from
 * `commerce.cutoff` so this page cannot disagree with the picker.
 */
import LegalLayout from "@components/LegalLayout.astro";
import { business, commerce, storeOpen } from "@config/site";

/* 20 becomes "8pm". Written once here, the same way the product page does it. */
const rawHour = commerce.cutoff.hour;
const cutoffHour = rawHour % 12 === 0 ? 12 : rawHour % 12;
const cutoffLabel = `${cutoffHour}${rawHour < 12 ? "am" : "pm"}`;
/* A number, not the config literal, so the plural survives a change. */
const daysBefore: number = commerce.cutoff.daysBefore;
const dayWord = daysBefore === 1 ? "day" : "days";

const TOC = [
  { id: "cancel", label: "Cancelling before the cutoff" },
  { id: "after-cutoff", label: "After the cutoff" },
  { id: "quality", label: "If something is wrong with the tray" },
  { id: "no-show", label: "Pickups nobody collects" },
  { id: "delivery", label: "Delivery and shipping" },
  { id: "how", label: "How to ask" },
  { id: "undecided", label: "What is not decided yet" },
];
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Refunds and cancellations"
  description="Gata is perishable and made to order. Cancel free until the cutoff for your bake date, and anything wrong with the tray is made right within 48 hours with a photograph."
  updated="2026-09-11"
  lede="Gata is perishable and it is made for your order, not taken off a shelf. That shapes everything below."
  status="draft"
  toc={TOC}
>
  <p slot="draft-note">
    {business.owner} has not signed this off yet. It is written out in full so
    that he can read a real page rather than a summary, and change whatever he
    disagrees with. Until he does, nobody will be held to it: if something goes
    wrong with an order, write to him and it gets sorted out.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cancel">Cancelling before the cutoff</h2>

  <p>
    Orders for a bake date close at <span class="tabular">{cutoffLabel}</span>,{" "}
    <span class="tabular">{daysBefore}</span> {dayWord} before that date. Up to
    that moment you can cancel for any reason, or no reason, and you get the
    full amount back. You do not have to explain.
  </p>

  <p>
    Changing an order (a different tray size, a different date) before the
    cutoff is the same thing: it is allowed, and it costs nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="after-cutoff">After the cutoff</h2>

  <p>
    Once the cutoff passes there is no refund for a change of mind. The
    ingredients for your tray have been bought and the batch is scheduled
    around it. Baking it and throwing it away costs the same as baking it and
    handing it over.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the whole reason for the rule. It is not a penalty, and it is why
    the cutoff sits {daysBefore} {dayWord} out rather than the night before.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quality">If something is wrong with the tray</h2>

  <div class="pullout">
    <p>
      <strong>Anything wrong with the food itself is made right.</strong> Send
      a photograph within 48 hours of collecting or receiving the tray and you
      get a replacement on the next bake date or your money back, whichever you
      prefer. There is no argument about it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The photograph is not a test. It is how {business.owner} finds out what
    went wrong, so he can tell whether it was the bake, the packing or the
    trip, and it is the only way to fix a problem he cannot see.
  </p>

  <p>
    The 48 hours are there because this is perishable food. Past that, nobody
    can tell whether a tray left out on a warm counter for a week was a
    problem in the kitchen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="no-show">Pickups nobody collects</h2>

  <p>
    A pickup order is held for 24 hours after the agreed time. After that it is
    not kept any longer and the order is not refunded. It is fresh food sitting
    in somebody's house, and holding it for longer would not do you any good
    either.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you are running late or something has happened, say so. A message before
    the pickup time is always easier to deal with than silence afterwards.
  </p>

  <h2 id="delivery">Delivery and shipping</h2>

  <p>
    For local delivery, somebody needs to be able to take the tray at the
    address you gave. If nobody is there, {business.owner} will contact you to
    sort out what happens next.
  </p>

  <p>
    For shipping inside California, a tray that arrives damaged is a quality
    problem and is handled the way the section above describes: a photograph
    within 48 hours of it arriving, and then a replacement or a refund. How
    long a parcel takes is the carrier's business, not ours, and a slow
    delivery is not by itself a refund.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">How to ask</h2>

  <p>
    Write to{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${business.email}?subject=Order%20question`}>{business.email}</a>{" "}
    with your order reference in the subject line, and say what you want to
    happen. There is no form to fill in and no ticket number. It is one person
    reading it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Refunds go back to the card that paid, through Stripe. That usually takes a
    few days to appear on a statement, and that part is the bank's timing
    rather than ours.
  </p>

  {
    !storeOpen && (
      <p class="unsettled">
        <span class="unsettled-title">Nothing can be ordered yet.</span> The
        store is not open, so nothing above applies to anybody today. It is
        published now so that it is readable before the first order rather than
        after it.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2 id="undecided">What is not decided yet</h2>

  <p>
    Two things in this policy are genuinely open, and guessing at them here
    would be worse than saying so.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Whether a missed local delivery is re-attempted, and whether that costs
      anything.
    </li>
    <li>
      Whether an order can be moved to a later bake date after the cutoff has
      passed, which is a kinder answer than a refusal but only if the kitchen
      can absorb it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Until {business.owner} decides, both are handled by asking him. He would
    rather answer an email than have a page make a promise he cannot keep.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
